        "titulo": "Saying your profession: no article, and the feminine in -in",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: with «sein» (and «werden»), the profession comes WITHOUT an article: «Ich bin Lehrer», and not «Ich bin ein Lehrer». STEP 2: the feminine form usually adds -in to the masculine: Lehrer → Lehrerin, Student → Studentin, Kellner → Kellnerin. STEP 3: some words get an umlaut: Arzt → Ärztin, Koch → Köchin. STEP 4: questions: «Was sind Sie von Beruf?» (formal), «Was bist du von Beruf?» (informal) or «Was machst du beruflich?». STEP 5: another way to say it: «Ich arbeite als Verkäufer» (I work as a salesperson), also without an article. Careful: «Krankenschwester» (nurse) has a different masculine: «Krankenpfleger».",
        "comparacao": "In English you say «I am a teacher», with «a». German does NOT use «ein» before a profession (unless there is an adjective with it). Only the feminine has its own form (-in).",
